d walk in this formation, blink in unison, and even perform the same gestures (if you hurt one of them, all three flinch). This is because they've actually be Drowned by a Deep One and are really one amorphous monstrosity: they've managed to scrape together some form of illusion to hide their hideous mass, but the magick is finnicky and requires multiple illusory bodies to hide the whole body.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>On to motives.</b><br /> First, I want to know more about this religion of yours that was the original body of the cult before Cottonwood took over. What was their goal in worship? What fears were they trying to allay or what changes were they trying to make to the world? Why was their faith so threatening that an Orobori den destroyed them? People turn to religion when they are suffering, and they choose what to worship based on how that suffering might be relieved or given purpose. Something about these gods gave the monks a reason to worship them: that reason could be a lie but we need to know what the lie might be before we can see the weight of the truth underneath it. Given the old gods are dreaming horrors, I doubt they pitched their goals straight.<br /> <br /> The concept of the twin gods bothers me primarily because one has a concept which is open to social interpretation (pleasure has the context to be a positive thing) while the other does not (nightmares are universally bad). I'd recommend making them BOTH gods of death and dreams: the first steals good dreams (with the cult believes are taken from the wicked and given to the devout) while the second eats bad dreams (thereby protecting the cult from the harm they can cause). Though the old gods both represent death, they give promise of consciousness eternal in the sea of dreams, floating blissfully on a gentle current at the eye of the storm, with non-believers dragged down into the swirling chaos. The cult also believes the twin gods will grant them the comfort of death in sleep: they have